  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cleveland's early assault buried the Giants before they could respond. Whisenhunt lasted four innings and gave away six runs; the bullpen. Harris and Sanmartin. Kept it from getting worse, but the game was over by the fourth. Eldridge hit one for show. The rest of the lineup struck out twelve times and went 0-for-6 with runners in scoring position. Nothing mattered after the first two innings.",
    "what_this_means": "This team doesn't recover from early damage anymore. They've left thirteen runners on base in two straight games while their starters keep exiting early. 4.0 innings tonight, a pattern that is becoming impossible to ignore. Cleveland scored six before anyone was warm. Harris struck out five across two innings, and Sanmartin was clean, but getting to those relievers required already trailing by the run differential of a full game. The inconsistency that defined the season showed up again, sharper: when Whisenhunt struggles, no one else can compensate. Left on base. No reward.",
    "what_to_watch": "Parker Messick arrives tonight with a 2.59 ERA, but Cleveland's offense is batting .233. A rare opening for Wilkinson to control the early frames and let the lineup find its rhythm while he's still sharp.",
